  "answer_markdown": "GreptimeDB was found by its historical name `greptimedb/greptimedb`: in the supplied catalog snapshot dated 2026-09-01, this alias is linked to [GreptimeTeam/greptimedb](https://github.com/GreptimeTeam/greptimedb). I recommend it only as a subject for further source verification: the search goal has been met, but suitability for adoption has not been established. The card classifies the project under Database Engines; its language, technical stack, interfaces, license and compatibility remain unknown. First place in decision_order reflects the host's preference for the stated goal and does not prove capabilities or compatibility.\n\nThe proposed validation architecture is a synthetic dataset → an isolated validation interface → GreptimeDB, if the selected version and its documentation confirm the required connection method. This is a hypothesis for a future trial, rather than an established data flow or an executed integration. The currently confirmed benefit is resolving the ambiguity of the historical name; potential product benefits from storing and processing data depend on a workload that has not yet been specified. The team must define operations and expected results, check the platform, dependencies, license, error handling and maintenance. In an equivalent-scope comparison, a ready-made component will require integration and maintenance, a custom implementation will additionally require implementing the required behavior, and the existing/native path will require checking the same requirements. The existing path is unknown, so an advantage, the complexity of full adoption and savings have not been established.\n\nGreptimeDB's activity remains unknown: `last_commit_at`, `pushed_at`, `repository_updated_at`, `last_release_at` and `observed_at` are missing; `last_synced_at=2026-08-11T13:41:45Z` indicates synchronization, rather than development activity. Unknown or truncated fields do not mean capabilities are absent. All 12 available card